WHAT THE ATTRIBUTION FILE COVERS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The whole production dependency closure, not only what survives tree-shaking
into dist/. Deriving the latter would mean parsing build output and would
under-report the moment the bundler changed, so the file is deliberately
generous: over-attributing costs a longer list, under-attributing is a
breach.

Two consequences of that choice are visible in the list and are not mistakes:

  - The twelve per-platform native builds of @napi-rs/canvas all appear. Any
    given installation resolves one of them, but which one depends on the
    machine.
  - @types/three appears although it contributes no runtime code. It is
    declared as a production dependency, so it is attributed as one.

Development-only dependencies — the Angular CLI, the TypeScript compiler,
Vitest, Playwright and their trees — are excluded. They are not redistributed
and carry no attribution obligation.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NO REMOTELY LOADED CODE
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Every third-party library is declared in package.json and bundled from the
registry copy. Nothing is fetched from a CDN at runtime, which is enforced by
scripts/check-no-remote-code.mjs rather than by convention.

This matters for attribution as well as for security: code arriving from a
CDN at runtime would be redistributed to the user without appearing in any
dependency manifest, so nothing would attribute it.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FONTS, ICONS AND IMAGES
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Fonts: none. The interface uses the reader's system font stack, and no font
file is bundled or fetched.

Interface icons: the viewer's own inline SVG path data (src/viewer-core/
icon.component.ts). No third-party icon set is bundled.

Application icons: the eight PNGs in public/icons, referenced by
manifest.webmanifest, are **the Angular logo** — the defaults left in place
from `ng generate @angular/pwa`. They are a third-party trademark presented
as this product's identity, which section 17.4 does not permit, and they must
be replaced with the product's own mark before release. Recorded as an open
finding in docs/licences.md.

Any addition of an asset is a licence decision under CLAUDE.md section 17.1,
which requires a documented, permissive licence recorded in docs/licences.md
before it enters the repository.

